  "slug": "bristol-myers-squibb",
  "summary": "Bristol Myers Squibb (BMS), headquartered in Princeton, New Jersey, is a multinational pharmaceutical company. In 2025, 69% of its revenue came from the United States. Its leading products included Eliquis for atrial fibrillation (30% of revenue), Opdivo for cancer (21%), Orencia for autoimmune diseases (8%), and Revlimid, Pomalyst, Yervoy and Reblozyl (5–6% each).\n\nBMS formed in 1989 through the merger of Bristol-Myers, founded in 1887, and Squibb, founded in 1858. Beginning in 2007, it shifted toward specialty biopharmaceuticals. Major acquisitions included Celgene for $74 billion in 2019, Turning Point Therapeutics for $4.1 billion in 2022, and Mirati, RayzeBio and Karuna Therapeutics in 2024. The FDA approved Cobenfy, acquired through Karuna, for schizophrenia in 2024.\n\nThe company has faced legal and accounting disputes, including settlements over Taxol and generic-drug competition, and a 2002 accounting scandal involving channel stuffing.",
